# bug-nilpy-one-line-def-and-class-bodies-do-not-parse — the IMPORTED-MODULE half.
#
# The main-file half landed in a843c17d4, which normalises Python's one-line
# suite in the LEXER: when a logical line opens with `def`/`class` and its
# depth-0 `:` is followed by real code, emit NEWLINE INDENT after the colon and
# DEDENT after the line's newline, so every token-level body scanner
# (PyRegisterDefShells, PyCollectModuleLocalsAST, PyScanDefGlobals) sees the
# shape it already understands.
#
# That rule fires only when `def`/`class` opens the logical line, which was
# tested as "TokCount = 0, or the previous token is a line break". Both clauses
# are about the WHOLE token stream — but PyLexAppend lexes an imported `.py`
# module on top of the importing program's tokens. At the module's first token
# TokCount is not 0, and the previous token is the MAIN file's tkEOF, so a
# one-line def on a module's first line was not recognised and the module failed
# with a bare "unexpected token" (reported against the importing file's line
# numbering, which is why it pointed at an unrelated line).
#
# Measured before fixing: the same one-line def one line FURTHER DOWN the module
# compiled and ran correctly, which is what isolated the guard rather than the
# normalisation itself.
#
# Diffed against CPython (tools/pydiff.py).

import nilpy_onelinemod

# the bug: a one-line def as the module's very first logical line
print(nilpy_onelinemod.get())

# controls — these already worked, and must keep working
print(nilpy_onelinemod.CONST)
print(nilpy_onelinemod.later())
print(nilpy_onelinemod.indented())

# a one-line `class C: pass` in a module, and an instance of it
k = nilpy_onelinemod.mk()
print(type(k).__name__)

# one-line methods inside an ordinary indented class body in a module
h = nilpy_onelinemod.Holder(21)
print(h.v, h.doubled())
